---
title: Parallel execution
intent: Prove four requests really overlap using a server-side rendezvous.
group: core
features:
- parallel
command: curlew run testapi/collections/parallel/60-barrier.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID" --parallel
order: 30
runnable: true
---

The barrier releases only after all four clients arrive. Run with `--parallel`: sequential execution cannot satisfy this fixture. For a dependency plan, use `--show-dependencies --dry-run`; it does not resolve future extraction values.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/parallel/60-barrier.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID" --parallel
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/parallel/60-barrier.yaml -->
```yaml
# Proof that --parallel is parallel.
#
# THIS COLLECTION REQUIRES --parallel. Run serially it fails, and that is the
# design: the barrier releases only when four requests are in flight at once, so
# a serial run blocks alone until the deadline and reports how many arrived.
#
# The alternative — comparing wall-clock durations between a serial and a
# parallel run — is what this repository already had to delete as flaky
# (M21-003). A rendezvous is a positive proof; a stopwatch is an inference.
#
#   curlew run 'testapi/collections/parallel/*.yaml' --env local --parallel
name: Parallelism, proven by rendezvous
description: |
  Four independent requests. Dependency analysis puts them in one wave, so
  --parallel runs them concurrently and the barrier releases. Without the flag
  they run in file order and the first one waits alone.

requests:
  - name: worker one reaches the barrier
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-barrier/barrier/4?timeout_ms=15000"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.expected: { equals: 4 }
        $.arrived: { equals: 4 }
        $.released: { equals: true }

  - name: worker two reaches the barrier
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-barrier/barrier/4?timeout_ms=15000"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.released: { equals: true }

  - name: worker three reaches the barrier
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-barrier/barrier/4?timeout_ms=15000"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.released: { equals: true }

  - name: worker four reaches the barrier
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-barrier/barrier/4?timeout_ms=15000"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.released: { equals: true }
        # Every arrival index between 1 and 4 is taken exactly once across the
        # four responses; each request only sees its own.
        $.arrival_index: { greater_than_or_equal: 1 }
```
